---
# vendir.yml for `muse` — python.
#
# Proven by running vendir 0.46.2 against anywaye/core: the file this produces is
# byte-identical (sha256) to the copy muse has committed today, at
# src/muse/schemas/telemetry/traces.schema.json.
#
# WHY `newRootPath` IS HERE. core keeps the document at
# `schemas/telemetry/traces.schema.json`; muse keeps it at
# `src/muse/schemas/telemetry/traces.schema.json`. vendir copies paths, so
# without a root change the file would land at
# `src/muse/schemas/telemetry/schemas/telemetry/traces.schema.json` and muse's
# own `from muse.schemas.telemetry...` would find nothing. `newRootPath` strips
# the upstream prefix. This is the same mechanism the courier case needs — the
# asymmetry where a document is not at the conventional path — which is why it
# is in the template and not in each repository's head.
#
# WHY `includePaths` NAMES A FILE, NOT A DIRECTORY. `includePaths` entries are
# joined to the sync root and matched with an exact glob against each file
# (`filepath.Join(root, pattern)` then `doublestar.PathMatch`). A pattern of
# `schemas/telemetry` matches the DIRECTORY and therefore no file inside it, and
# vendir fails with "Expected to find at least one file within directory" — a
# loud failure, which is the good case. The dangerous case is the pattern in the
# wrong place entirely: see core/vendir/README.md, "The trap that is not loud".
#
# WHY `ref` IS A TAG AND NOT `master`. A branch produces a PR nobody reads and a
# diff nobody reviews. core does not publish tags yet, so this file says `master`
# and MUST be changed to a `vX.Y.Z` tag in the same commit that adds the first
# tag. See core/release/README.md.
apiVersion: vendir.k14s.io/v1alpha1
kind: Config
minimumRequiredVersion: 0.32.0
directories:
  - path: src/muse/schemas/telemetry
    contents:
      - path: .
        includePaths:
          - schemas/telemetry/traces.schema.json
        newRootPath: schemas/telemetry
        git:
          url: https://github.com/anywaye/core.git
          ref: master
